se,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":4,"created_at":"2025-08-27T05:54:37Z","updated_at":"2025-09-01T12:23:34Z","closed_at":"2025-08-31T08:01:51Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nI only tested this in 4.4.1 stable official\n\n### System information\n\nWindows 11, I'm using an acer swift 14 Go, this doesn't seem to be a thing that would be dependent on specs though.\n\n### Issue description\n\nWhen trying to use an animation player to control what animation is playing in an animated sprite 2d it causes some unintended behavior. This is closer to an exploit than a bug. The fact that changing a sprite property opens up the sprite frames tab works weirdly when that property is being edited by the animation player. It causes the sprite frame tab to open when it goes over the keyframe but in the animation tab the cursor is binded to the mouse so you can't move the mouse without changing the animation.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\ncreate a animated sprite 2d with 2 or more animations and attempt to keyframe the current animation property in the keyframe. do that a second time and run the animation.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nI don't have the time to do this but it seems so simple to reproduce. Sorry though.","closed_by":{"login":"istubedmytoe","id":209389765,"node_id":"U_kgDODHsIxQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/209389765?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/istubedmytoe","html_url":"https://github.com/istubedmytoe","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/istubedmytoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/istubedmytoe/follo